gold_answer:
Depending on what you mean by

> such that the resolution loss increases with increasing ` uint16 ` values

you might want to try different compression functions. Without any further
information about the context, we will assume that you are looking for an
increasing [ concave function
](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concave_function) to map [0~65535] to [0~255].

You can try first the function ` sqrt ` :



    def sqrt_compress(x: np.uint16) -> np.uint8:
        return np.uint8(math.sqrt(x))


If the smoothness of this function is not exactly what you need, you can try
other powers rather that 1/2 for the square root. You can add the power as a
parameter ` p ` that will have effect on the smoothness of the compression
function:



    def power_compress_p(x: np.uint16, p: float = 2) -> np.uint8:
        """Compression function. Higher value of p use less bits."""
        return np.uint8(2 ** (8 - 16 / p) * x ** (1 / p))


For your use case, use ` p > 1 ` .

The power functions obviously are not the only compression that exist, you
could use a dictionary to define a map function if you want better control
over the granularity.

